{#
  Premium, said plainly, the way a cookbook prints its price on the back cover. The
  amounts are the App Store catalogue's US prices (the app itself never prints a
  price: the store does, in the reader's currency), so the page says which currency
  it means and where the local price is shown. It also says up front that
  FlavorDraft is a subscription, as the app is after onboarding. No paper here:
  pricing is information, not a piece of the cookbook.
#}
<section class="pricing" id="pricing" aria-labelledby="pricing-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="pricing__body">
      <h2 class="pricing__title" id="pricing-title">FlavorDraft Premium</h2>
      <p class="pricing__text">FlavorDraft is a subscription. You can set up your cookbook and try a first import before you choose a plan.</p>
      <p class="pricing__text">It is <b class="price">US$3.99 a month</b>, starting with a <span class="nowrap">7-day</span> free trial for eligible users, or <b class="price">US$29.99 a year</b>, with no free trial. Both include every import method, unlimited AI drafts and all your collections.</p>
      <p class="pricing__fine">Prices here are in US dollars. Before you subscribe, FlavorDraft and the <span class="nowrap">App Store</span> show the price in your currency, which can vary by country or region. Subscriptions renew automatically until you cancel them in Settings. <a class="nowrap" href="/support/#subscriptions">Managing your subscription</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
